Subject: winex11: navigate with a middle-button drag

Live drives arrangement zoom from Ctrl+wheel and has no function on the
middle button. Other DAWs put navigation there: holding the middle button
and moving the pointer scrolls the arrangement in both directions without
reaching for a scrollbar (issue 50).

WINE_X11_MIDDLE_DRAG=navigate holds a middle-button press back from the
application and reports the movement that follows as wheel input at the
position the press landed on. Vertical movement becomes wheel, horizontal
movement becomes horizontal wheel, one whole notch per 24 raw virtual
screen pixels: map_event_coords() reports MDT_RAW_DPI coordinates with no
DPI normalization, so one notch is a fixed physical distance and covers
less of a window's content the more the display is scaled up. A press that
moves no further than 3 raw pixels is replayed as a plain middle click on
release, the down stamped with the time and at the position the press
landed on, so the button keeps its normal reporting when it is not used
for a drag.

The report follows the pointer continuously rather than waiting for a whole
notch: WHEEL_DELTA is a unit, not a minimum, and a precision pointing device
sends part of one too. WINE_X11_MIDDLE_DRAG=notch rounds to whole notches
for an application that ignores the rest.

A press of another button during a drag ends it: a drag that reported
wheel movement syncs the cursor to the real pointer position, a press that
never moved is replayed as a click, and the press and the movement that
follows go through, so placing the cursor with the left button while
panning with the middle one does not leave a phantom click behind. A drag
whose release was lost ends the same way on the next event.

No modifier is added. A Ctrl the user holds during the drag still reaches
the message through the desktop key state, so the same drag performs
whatever the application does for Ctrl+wheel.

The default is off, where the middle button reports WM_MBUTTONDOWN and
WM_MBUTTONUP exactly as before.

The drag state is process-global while winex11 polls each thread's display
on that thread, so drags on windows owned by different threads (Live's
main window and a bridged plugin window) can race on it. A stale drag left
by a lost release is flushed before a new one begins, which keeps the
common single-pointer case in order.
